27
00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.690
Stories are about conflict and resolution.

28
00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.930
Stories universally address three big questions.

29
00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.770
Origin, who is the character and where is he or she from?

30
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.770
Conflict, what is the problem? What went wrong?

31
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.270
And resolution, how do we fix it?

32
00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:39.210
Even though stories vary on the particulars, the specific character, the setting,

33
00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:45.130
the time frame, the problem and how they fix it, all stories address these three big

34
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:50.750
questions. whether a movie, a novel, a commercial, or even a personal

35
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.910
anecdote from a friend. All stories share this basic underlying structure.

36
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.630
In fact, you can't tell a story without it.

37
00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:05.510
So what worldview best accounts for our storytelling nature and for the objective

38
00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.690
structure of storytelling we find in the world?

39
00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.550
